### 1. Architectural Question: Should We Keep Sign-Up?

> **Verdict: NO, do NOT keep mandatory sign-up.**
> 
> In physical real-world party and action games (e.g. *Among Us*, *Spaceteam*, *Tag*, *Jackbox*), requiring players to register with email, passwords, and verification links creates **immediate drop-off and friction**. Friends standing together in a park or campus just want to jump into a game in 3 seconds.
>
> **How we have structured it (The Zero-Friction Model):**
> 1. **Codename + 🎲 Random Button**: Operative enters a tactical codename (or taps 🎲 for an instant name like *Phantom*, *Nomad*, *Vesper*).
> 2. **Instant Room Host / Join**: Enter the 6-letter room code (or host) and you are in the lobby immediately.
> 3. **Persistent Identity**: The app saves your codename and session token in `localStorage`, so if your phone turns off or signal drops, you reconnect automatically to your active game without losing your spot.

